journal-package/
journal-package.tar.gz
journal-package.zip

# Build marker (internal make state)
.make_build_marker

# ── Private maintainer overlay (see also .dockerignore, JOURNAL_EXCLUDE) ──────
# Real files live in `private/`, a nested, SEPARATE git repo (gitignored here).
# Gitignored symlinks at the public root point into it; set up / repaired by
# `make private-init` (scripts/init-private.sh). No trailing slashes, so each
# pattern matches the SYMLINK (which git sees as a file) as well as a real
# file/dir. These sit AFTER the `!*.md` / `!*.json` / `!*/` includes above, so
# they re-ignore the symlinks that those includes would otherwise track.
# `private` likewise overrides `!*/` so git never descends into the private repo.
private
dev-notes
COAUTHOR_SETUP.md

# AI-tool files — kept entirely in private/ai/. The public repo ships no AI
# guidance, settings, or rule files. `make private-init` creates gitignored
# symlinks at the canonical paths so the maintainer's tools find their config.
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md
.claude
.github/copilot-instructions.md
.aider*
.cursor*
.tabnine

# Maintainer-only docs that sit inside otherwise-public dirs (symlinked from
# private/ when present). Listed explicitly so they're ignored either way.
docs/NOTEBOOK_SUPPORT_IMPLEMENTATION.md
tests/NOTEBOOK_TESTS_SUMMARY.md
tests/TEST_IMPROVEMENTS.md

# Environment directories (generated, platform-specific)
.venv/
.env/
.julia/
.cache/
# .stata/ is generated -- EXCEPT ado/plus, which is COMMITTED. SSC serves only
# the current release and has no versioned install, so the only way to pin these
# packages is to ship them: "provide copies of such packages/modules when the
# package repository does not allow you to specify a version" (AEA Data Editor
# guidance). Refresh deliberately with `make stata-update`, never automatically.
#
# Without these five lines the vendoring mechanism is inert: env/Makefile would
# install into a directory git refuses to track, `make stata-check` would verify
# a tree no clone ever receives, and the pin would exist only on the machine that
# happened to create it.
.stata/*
!.stata/ado/
.stata/ado/*
!.stata/ado/plus/
!.stata/ado/plus/**
# env/Manifest.toml is NOT committed in the TEMPLATE, and that is a considered
# reversal of 2026-08-15 rather than an oversight.
#
# A Julia Manifest pins packages only for the Julia version it was resolved with.
# On any other Julia, Pkg does not fail -- it rewrites the manifest and carries
# on. And a template cannot control which Julia a machine can install: juliacall
# declares OpenSSL_jll as "<=python", tying the maximum usable Julia to the host
# Python's OpenSSL, so a manifest resolved on 1.12 here is unusable on a GitHub
# runner capped at 1.11.
#
# Committing it therefore meant CI printed "NOT REPRODUCING THE PINNED VERSIONS"
# on every single run -- a warning that always fires is one people learn to
# ignore, which is worse than not having it.
#
# YOUR project should commit it. It knows its platforms; a template does not.
# The machinery is all here and stays: install_julia.py restores the manifest
# rather than deleting it, julia-instantiate reports whether the pin survived,
# and `make julia-relock` regenerates deliberately. Add the line back once you
# have run `make environment` on the machines you care about:
#
#     !/env/Manifest.toml
#
# An explicit ignore is required, not merely the absence of a "!" line: the
# allowlist above has a general `!*.toml`, which would otherwise track this file
# anyway. (That also means the `!/env/Manifest.toml` line added on 2026-08-15 was
# redundant from the start -- `!*.toml` had already allowed it.)
/env/Manifest.toml
env/lock.pid

